#       sh is this shell's third name, the one /bin/sh scripts meet: neither
#       bash nor dash, it follows POSIX where the two part -- getopts leaves
#       OPTARG unset beside an option that takes none and does not rewind
#       for set --, as bash --posix does and dash does not -- so bash
#       --posix is what it answers to, run under that name with no flag of
#       its own. It is walked only by the families that name it.
